course: dictionary
lesson: 13176
title: Fraudulent: the word for a trick
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Fraudulent means dishonest, and it is meant to trick people, usually to get their money. Context clues help you find it: «The email said you had won a prize, but first you must send your password.» Nobody gives away prizes for passwords, so the clues show that the email is a trick, which means it is fraudulent.
explain: Fraudulent can also mean fake or forged, pretending to be real. «The shop sold fraudulent tickets that looked real but were printed by a cheat.» The tickets look genuine, but they are not. Always check: put "fake or a trick" into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Be careful with messages that ask for passwords or money. If a message seems fraudulent, do not click any links or reply. Stop, and tell a grown-up you trust.
fact: Fraudulent means dishonest and meant to trick people.
fact: «Fraudulent» often describes a scam that tries to get money.
fact: Fraudulent can also mean fake or forged, pretending to be real.
fact: «Fraudulent emails» and «fraudulent websites» are common examples in the news.
fact: If a message asks for your password or money, it may be fraudulent, so be careful.
exercise: «The email said you had won a prize, but you must send your password first.» What is the email? | An honest letter from your teacher | *A trick to get your password | A birthday card from a friend
exercise: «The shop sold fraudulent tickets that looked real but were fake.» What does fraudulent mean here? | Brand new and shiny | Cheap but honest | *Fake or forged, pretending to be real
exercise: «The fraudulent website asked for money.» Find and copy the word that tells you the website is a trick. | *fraudulent | money | website | asked
test: Priya gets a message: «Click here to claim your free gift and type in your bank details.» Why is it fraudulent? | It is written in capital letters | *It asks for your bank details to trick you, and free gifts like this are often scams | It asks for your bank details to trick you | It is sent by a teacher
test: What does fraudulent mean? | Quick and tidy | *Dishonest, meant to trick people | Very old and worn | Happy and friendly
exam: A message says «Your account is blocked, send money now.» What is the safest thing to do? | *Do not reply or click, and tell a grown-up, because it may be fraudulent | Reply and send the money quickly | Click the link and type your password | Forward it to all your friends
